* SETAI
* SET.AI verb: whether the AI may be used at all, which AI provider it uses, its model and key, the custom connection, the certificate authority file and the limits
* (S.69 step 2, carried to Solo as carry 15; the owner's decisions of 23 Oct 2026).
* Copyright (c) 2026, SD Core Solo for Linux.  This program is free software; you can redistribute
* it and/or modify it under the terms of the GNU General Public License, version 3 or later.
*
* START-HISTORY:
* 18 Nov 26 SD Core Solo - four-version audit round 3 (P-5126): the typed key's client status is kept in key.stat and tested, as Linux Solo.
* 17 Nov 26 SD Core Solo - four-version audit, Linux Solo 3b1316cc (as SD Core for Windows c9950057): SET.AI KEY reads the client's exit
*               status (os.error(), "; exit $LASTEXITCODE" on the command) after both forms; a client that failed no longer reports an earlier
*               key as set (12613 and nothing audited, before the listing; the hand-over file is still dropped first).
* 16 Nov 26 Windows port - from Linux Solo c8dac7a: the AI gate message is 15925 (was 12611; Linux Solo 14925), text unchanged;
*               SD Core's 12611 is another message.
* 11 Nov 26 dm  Carried from SD Core (owner, 11 Nov 2026: "Carry Core's dialog"): google is a provider and MODEL - goes back to the provider's starting model (e1061f73);
*               SET.AI MODELS provider lists the provider's own model names; SET.AI MODEL warns (15016, 15018) when the provider does not list the name; SET.AI KEY provider
*               HANDOVER takes the key from the AI page's hand-over file ($aikey/key in the Solo tree, a directory of mode 0700) and removes the file whatever happens;
*               run.ctl no longer takes a helper's refusal whose words hold "not found" for an unreachable helper (S.170, 2cced06c).
* 11 Nov 26 dm  Carried from SD Core (S.160, 7 Nov 26, 74cf7001): SET.AI CONTEXT hex | - (the standing text sent first with every request), put to the wall's screen
*               before the helper keeps it; messages 15011-15014 (shared with Core) and 12614's usage.  The code of the case is Core's.
* 23 Oct 26 dm  Solo carry 15: the helper's client is <tree>/tools/sd-ai-ctl (Core: root-owned under /usr/local/lib); the word GATE ON | OFF is Solo's account gate (Core's is
*               modify.account NAME ai-on, one per account); message 15925 says it.  Everything else is Core's program.
* 23 Oct 26 dm  (Core) New (S.69 step 2; the owner: "an ai choice page ... a checkbox for the currently selected provider ... the same model needs to be used by all accounts").
* 16 Nov 26 SD Core Solo - RELEASE_1.1 246: from Linux Solo 415b9f3 (its same-user form, c89bb9b, 6941038, f802f30, 10fcf2f), with its messages renumbered
*              (11xxx + 1005, 14xxx + 1000) and the Windows facts of the client, as SD Core for Windows' setai: the client is the helper's own program,
*              <tree>\usr\bin\sd-ai.exe (K$WINPATH of /usr/bin/sd-ai.exe), which is the client when its first word is one of the client's; OS.EXECUTE runs
*              PowerShell here, so it is called as & '<path>' 'word' ..., its path is refused only for a single quote, nothing is redirected and the carriage
*              returns are taken out of what it printed; KEY p HANDOVER gives it the hand-over file's path (--handover: PowerShell has no < redirection) and the
*              verb removes the file again with Remove-Item.  12614's usage says ca C:/PATH (a word here may not hold a backslash).
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   SET.AI                                   says what is set
*   SET.AI GATE on | off                     whether the AI may be used at all (off as delivered); the one switch Solo's single account has
*   SET.AI PROVIDER name | none              the provider every account uses: anthropic, openai, google, deepseek or custom; none turns the AI off
*   SET.AI MODEL provider name | -           the model for a provider (- clears it: the provider's starting model is used again; Custom has none)
*   SET.AI KEY provider                      asks for the key at the terminal, not shown, and keeps it for the helper only
*   SET.AI KEY provider CLEAR                removes it
*   SET.AI KEY provider HANDOVER             the key the AI page's setup dialog wrote to its hand-over file ($aikey\key in the sdsys directory, a directory only the Solo
*                                            user and SYSTEM may enter); the client reads the file and removes it, and the verb removes it again whatever happened
*   SET.AI MODELS provider                   the provider's own list of model names, asked with its key
*   SET.AI CUSTOM name url format auth version | - maxparam     the fourth choice, where everything is the administrator's (format anthropic | openai, auth bearer | x-api-key)
*   SET.AI CUSTOM CLEAR                      forgets the custom connection (not while it is the selected provider)
*   SET.AI CA path | -                       the file of certificate authorities for a private CA (- is the system store).  On Windows the path is written with forward
*                                            slashes, C:/Users/me/ca.pem: a word here may not hold a backslash, and the helper reads either form
*   SET.AI LIMIT name number                 requests-hour tokens-day timeout bytes-request bytes-reply max-tokens
*   SET.AI CONTEXT hex | -                   the standing text sent with every request, as hexadecimal ASCII (tool.ai's T builds it); - is the shipped default
*
*   The administrator's verb (the AI choice page, tool.ai, runs it).  It changes nothing itself: the settings live in the AI helper's own directory, and the helper
*   (sd-ai.exe) makes the change - this verb runs the helper's client, sd-ai.exe itself, as the administrator.  In Solo the helper runs as the Solo user and cannot tell
*   this verb from any other process of that user; the administrator test is SD's, here.
*   SET.AI KEY hands the console to the client, which reads the key itself without echo: that key never passes through SD.  The AI page's setup dialog (Core S.170, the
*   owner's choice after a warning; carried 11 Nov 2026) reads the key in SD's session and hands it over in a file in a directory only the Solo user and SYSTEM may
*   enter; neither form puts the key on a
*   command line or in the audit trail.  One provider and one model serve the account;
*   whether the account may use it is the gate (set.ai gate on).  Nothing is selected, no model or key is set and the gate is off as delivered, so the AI is off until the
*   administrator has done all of that.  A URL for custom is https only, with no query string.  Every change is audited in lower case, with no key in it.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
program set.ai
$catalog $setai

$include int$keys.h
$include keys.h
$include err.h

   @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS

   if not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)) then
      stop sysmsg(2001) ;* Command requires administrator privileges
   end

   okc = 'ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789._:/@+,-'
*  The helper's client is the helper's own program, sd-ai.exe in the Solo tree's usr\bin beside sd.exe (the one the kernel starts for a request), which is the client
*  when its first word is one of the client's.  The administrator's own environment may name another, for the tests only (SD_AI_CTL); the product never sets it.  The
*  path may have spaces and backslashes, so it is not held to okc: it goes inside single quotes on the PowerShell command line, and the one character that could end
*  those quotes is refused.
   ctl = env('SD_AI_CTL')
   if ctl = '' then ctl = kernel(K$WINPATH, '/usr/bin/sd-ai.exe')
   if ctl = '' or index(ctl, "'", 1) then stop sysmsg(12612)       ;* no client path, or one a command line may not carry

   words = trim(@sentence)
   n.words = dcount(words, ' ')
   for i = 2 to n.words
      w = field(words, ' ', i)
      if trim(convert(okc, space(len(okc)), w)) # '' then stop sysmsg(12627, w[1, 40])    ;* %1 has a character that is not allowed here
   next i
   sub = downcase(field(words, ' ', 2))
   providers = ' anthropic openai google deepseek custom '

   begin case
      case n.words = 1
         qargs = "'show'"
         gosub run.ctl
         for i = 1 to dcount(out, @fm)
            display out<i>
         next i
         @system.return.code = 0

      case sub = 'gate' and n.words = 3 and (downcase(field(words, ' ', 3)) = 'on' or downcase(field(words, ' ', 3)) = 'off')
         gstate = downcase(field(words, ' ', 3))
         qargs = "'gate' '" : gstate : "'"
         gosub run.ctl
         gosub need.ok
         void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'set.ai gate=' : gstate)
         display sysmsg(15925, gstate)                                                    ;* The AI gate is now %1
         @system.return.code = 0

      case sub = 'provider' and n.words = 3
         prov = downcase(field(words, ' ', 3))
         if prov # 'none' and index(providers, ' ' : prov : ' ', 1) = 0 then stop sysmsg(12615, prov[1, 40])      ;* %1 is not one of anthropic, openai, google, deepseek and custom
         qargs = "'provider' '" : prov : "'"
         gosub run.ctl
         gosub need.ok
         void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'set.ai provider=' : prov)
         if prov = 'none' then display sysmsg(12617) else display sysmsg(12616, prov)       ;* The AI is now off ... / The AI provider for every account is now %1
         @system.return.code = 0

      case sub = 'model' and n.words = 4
         prov = downcase(field(words, ' ', 3))
         model = field(words, ' ', 4)
         if index(providers, ' ' : prov : ' ', 1) = 0 then stop sysmsg(12615, prov[1, 40])
         qargs = "'model' '" : prov : "' '" : model : "'"
         gosub run.ctl
         gosub need.ok
         if model = '-' then
            void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'set.ai model provider=' : prov : ' cleared')
            display sysmsg(12619, prov)                                                   ;* The model for %1 is cleared
         end else
            void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'set.ai model provider=' : prov : ' model=' : model)
            display sysmsg(12618, prov, model)                                            ;* The model for %1 is now %2
*           Core S.170: the name is kept either way (a provider's list is not always complete), but a name the provider does not list is said, with the one it lists that
*           differs only in case.  No list (no key yet, a provider with none, the network) says nothing.
            gosub list.models
            if ml.ok and ml.n > 0 then
               gosub find.model
               if not(fm.found) then
                  display sysmsg(15016, prov, model)                                      ;* %1 does not list a model named %2; requests may be refused
                  if fm.near # '' then display sysmsg(15018, fm.near)                     ;* It lists %1
               end
            end
         end
         @system.return.code = 0

      case sub = 'models' and n.words = 3
         prov = downcase(field(words, ' ', 3))
         if index(providers, ' ' : prov : ' ', 1) = 0 then stop sysmsg(12615, prov[1, 40])
         gosub list.models
         if not(ml.ok) then
            @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
            stop sysmsg(12613, ml.code, ml.words[1, 120])                                 ;* The AI helper refused: %1 (%2)
         end
         display sysmsg(15017, prov, ml.n)                                                ;* %1 lists %2 models:
         for i = 1 to ml.n
            display '  ' : ml.list<i>
         next i
         @system.return.code = 0

      case sub = 'key' and n.words = 4 and downcase(field(words, ' ', 4)) = 'handover'
         prov = downcase(field(words, ' ', 3))
         hand.f = @sdsys : @ds : '$aikey' : @ds : 'key'
         if index(providers, ' ' : prov : ' ', 1) = 0 then
            gosub drop.handover
            stop sysmsg(12615, prov[1, 40])
         end
*        The client reads the key from the hand-over file itself (--handover: PowerShell has no < redirection to make the file its standard input), so the key is on no
*        command line; the client removes the file whatever happens, and so does this verb, BEFORE anything else is looked at (run.ctl's stops would otherwise leave it).
*        What happened is read back from the helper, as for the typed key; the client's own words are not trusted for it.
         out = ''
         os.execute "& '" : ctl : "' 'key' '" : prov : "' '--handover' '" : hand.f : "'; exit $LASTEXITCODE" capturing out
         key.stat = os.error()
         gosub drop.handover
*        17 Nov 26 (Linux Solo 3b1316cc; SD Core for Windows c9950057): the client's own exit status first.  A client that failed set
*        nothing, and a key stored EARLIER would still make the listing below say "key: set" - so a failure stops here, before the listing,
*        the audit line and 12620.  "; exit $LASTEXITCODE": OS.EXECUTE runs PowerShell, whose own status is not the program's.
         if key.stat # 0 then
            @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
            stop sysmsg(12613, 'nokey', 'the key was not set')                             ;* The AI helper refused: %1 (%2)
         end
         out = convert(char(13), '', out)                                                ;* PowerShell's line ends (see run.ctl)
         qargs = "'show'"
         gosub run.ctl
         gosub check.key
         if not(key.found) then
            @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
            stop sysmsg(12613, 'nokey', 'the key was not set')                             ;* The AI helper refused: %1 (%2)
         end
         void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'set.ai key set provider=' : prov : ' from=page')
         display sysmsg(12620, prov)                                                      ;* The key for %1 is set
         @system.return.code = 0

      case sub = 'key' and n.words = 4 and downcase(field(words, ' ', 4)) = 'clear'
         prov = downcase(field(words, ' ', 3))
         if index(providers, ' ' : prov : ' ', 1) = 0 then stop sysmsg(12615, prov[1, 40])
         qargs = "'key-clear' '" : prov : "'"
         gosub run.ctl
         gosub need.ok
         void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'set.ai key cleared provider=' : prov)
         display sysmsg(12621, prov)                                                      ;* The key for %1 is cleared
         @system.return.code = 0

      case sub = 'key' and n.words = 3
         prov = downcase(field(words, ' ', 3))
         if index(providers, ' ' : prov : ' ', 1) = 0 then stop sysmsg(12615, prov[1, 40])
*        The console is handed to the helper's client and nothing is captured: the key is typed there, never read by SD.  What happened is then read back, not assumed.
*        Nothing is redirected: the client writes to standard output only.
         os.execute "& '" : ctl : "' 'key' '" : prov : "'; exit $LASTEXITCODE"
         key.stat = os.error()
*        17 Nov 26: the client's exit status first, as in the hand-over form above (18 Nov 26, P-5126: kept in key.stat, as Linux Solo and SD Core).
         if key.stat # 0 then
            @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
            stop sysmsg(12613, 'nokey', 'the key was not set')                             ;* The AI helper refused: %1 (%2)
         end
         qargs = "'show'"
         gosub run.ctl
         gosub check.key
         if not(key.found) then
            @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
            stop sysmsg(12613, 'nokey', 'the key was not set')                             ;* The AI helper refused: %1 (%2)
         end
         void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'set.ai key set provider=' : prov)
         display sysmsg(12620, prov)                                                      ;* The key for %1 is set
         @system.return.code = 0

      case sub = 'custom' and n.words = 3 and downcase(field(words, ' ', 3)) = 'clear'
         qargs = "'custom-clear'"
         gosub run.ctl
         gosub need.ok
         void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'set.ai custom cleared')
         display sysmsg(12623)                                                            ;* The custom connection is cleared
         @system.return.code = 0

      case sub = 'custom' and n.words = 8
         cname = field(words, ' ', 3)
         curl = field(words, ' ', 4)
         qargs = "'custom'"
         for i = 3 to 8
            qargs := " '" : field(words, ' ', i) : "'"
         next i
         gosub run.ctl
         gosub need.ok
         chost = field(curl, '/', 3)
*        The audit line names the host and never the whole URL: nothing a person might put in a URL is written to the trail
         void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'set.ai custom name=' : cname : ' host=' : chost)
         display sysmsg(12622, cname, chost)                                              ;* The custom connection is now %1, at %2
         @system.return.code = 0

      case sub = 'ca' and n.words = 3
         cpath = field(words, ' ', 3)
         qargs = "'ca' '" : cpath : "'"
         gosub run.ctl
         gosub need.ok
         void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'set.ai ca file=' : cpath)
         if cpath = '-' then display sysmsg(12625) else display sysmsg(12624, cpath)      ;* The ca-file is now the system store ... / The ca-file is now %1
         @system.return.code = 0

      case sub = 'limit' and n.words = 4
         lname = downcase(field(words, ' ', 3))
         lvalue = field(words, ' ', 4)
         qargs = "'limit' '" : lname : "' '" : lvalue : "'"
         gosub run.ctl
         gosub need.ok
         void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'set.ai limit name=' : lname : ' value=' : lvalue)
         display sysmsg(12626, lname, lvalue)                                             ;* The limit %1 is now %2
         @system.return.code = 0

      case sub = 'context' and n.words = 3
*        11 Nov 26 (Core S.160): the standing text sent with every request, as ONE word of hexadecimal ASCII (a text of several lines is carried whole that way; tool.ai builds it), or -
*        for the shipped default.  The text is decoded here and put to the wall's screen BEFORE the helper is asked: a standing text that names SD's own code would go with every
*        request, so it is never kept.  The helper checks it again (printable ASCII and line feeds, 4000 characters).  The audit line has the size, never the text.
         hx = field(words, ' ', 3)
         if hx = '-' then
            qargs = "'context' '-'"
            gosub run.ctl
            gosub need.ok
            void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'set.ai context=default')
            display sysmsg(15012)                                                         ;* ... is the shipped default again
            @system.return.code = 0
            stop
         end
         hx = downcase(hx)
         if len(hx) = 0 or mod(len(hx), 2) or len(hx) > 8000 or convert('0123456789abcdef', '', hx) # '' then stop sysmsg(15014)
         ctext = iconv(hx, 'MX0C')
         if status() or len(ctext) * 2 # len(hx) then stop sysmsg(15014)
         for i = 1 to len(ctext)
            cc = seq(ctext[i, 1])
            if cc # 10 and (cc < 32 or cc > 126) then stop sysmsg(15014)
         next i
         call $tk_aiwall('request', ctext, why)
         if why # '' then
            void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'set.ai context refused name=' : why)
            stop sysmsg(15013, why)                                                       ;* ... names SD's own code or build (%1) and is not kept
         end
         qargs = "'context' '" : hx : "'"
         gosub run.ctl
         gosub need.ok
         void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'set.ai context bytes=' : len(ctext))
         display sysmsg(15011, len(ctext))                                                ;* ... is now the administrator's (%1 characters)
         @system.return.code = 0

      case 1
         display sysmsg(12614)                                                            ;* set.ai {provider NAME | ... }
   end case
   stop

* The helper's client, run as the administrator with its words already quoted; out holds what it printed (its errors too: the client writes everything to standard output,
* and nothing is redirected, so PowerShell adds no error records of its own).  A client that is not there or a helper that cannot be reached ends it with 12612, and a
* refusal with 12613 and the helper's own code and words.
run.ctl:
   out = ''
   os.execute "& '" : ctl : "' " : qargs capturing out
*  16 Nov 26 SD Core Solo, as SD Core for Windows: PowerShell ends every captured line with a carriage return, which "ok" and the [x] lines must not carry
   out = convert(char(13), '', out)
*  11 Nov 26 (Core S.170): a refusal of the helper's (error: ...) is not an unreachable helper, whatever its words hold: a provider's "model not found" passes through it now
   if trim(out) = '' or (trim(out<1>)[1, 7] # 'error: ' and (index(out, 'could not be reached', 1) or index(out, 'not found', 1) or index(out, 'No such file', 1) or index(out, 'Permission denied', 1))) then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      stop sysmsg(12612)                                                                  ;* The AI helper could not be reached ...
   end
   return

* After a setting: the client says ok (or, for a key, its own sentence), or error: code: words.
need.ok:
   first = trim(out<1>)
   if first[1, 7] = 'error: ' then
      rest = first[8, 999]
      code = field(rest, ':', 1)
      words2 = trim(rest[index(rest, ':', 1) + 1, 999])
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      stop sysmsg(12613, code, words2[1, 70])                                             ;* The AI helper refused: %1 (%2)
   end
   if first # 'ok' and first[1, 8] # 'The key ' then                                        ;* key-clear prints its own sentence
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      stop sysmsg(12613, 'badreply', first[1, 70])
   end
   return

* Core S.170: the provider's own list of model names (prov).  ml.ok 1 with ml.list (one to a field) and ml.n; ml.ok 0 with the helper's ml.code and ml.words.  run.ctl's
* stops still apply (a helper that cannot be reached ends the verb).
list.models:
   qargs = "'models' '" : prov : "'"
   gosub run.ctl
   ml.ok = 0 ; ml.list = '' ; ml.n = 0 ; ml.code = '' ; ml.words = ''
   first = trim(out<1>)
   if first[1, 7] = 'error: ' then
      rest = first[8, 999]
      ml.code = field(rest, ':', 1)
      ml.words = trim(rest[index(rest, ':', 1) + 1, 999])
      return
   end
   if first[1, 8] # 'models: ' then
      ml.code = 'badreply'
      ml.words = first[1, 70]
      return
   end
   ml.ok = 1
   for i = 2 to dcount(out, @fm)
      w = trim(out<i>)
      if w # '' then
         ml.n += 1
         ml.list<ml.n> = w
      end
   next i
   return

* fm.found 1 when model is in ml.list exactly; fm.near the listed name that differs from it only in case
find.model:
   fm.found = 0
   fm.near = ''
   for i = 1 to ml.n
      if ml.list<i> = model then fm.found = 1
      if fm.near = '' and downcase(ml.list<i>) = downcase(model) then fm.near = ml.list<i>
   next i
   if fm.found then fm.near = ''
   return

* Core S.170: the hand-over file goes, whatever happened (single quotes: the $ of $aikey is not a PowerShell variable there)
drop.handover:
   os.execute "Remove-Item -LiteralPath '" : hand.f : "' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue"
   return

* Whether the show listing (in out) says a key is set for the provider: its block is the line that names it and the line after it
check.key:
   key.found = 0
   for i = 1 to dcount(out, @fm)
      ln = trim(out<i>)
      if (ln[1, 3] = '[x]' or ln[1, 3] = '[ ]') and index(ln, ' ' : prov : ' ', 1) then
         if index(out<i + 1>, 'key: set', 1) then key.found = 1
      end
   next i
   return

end

* END-CODE
